course: everyday
lesson: 48
title: Weather emergencies
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: Every region has its own dangerous weather, and families get ready by having a plan, an emergency kit with water, food, a torch and a radio, and by listening to official warnings.
explain: A tornado is a spinning column of air that can flatten houses; in the US Midwest and the Great Plains, when a tornado warning sounds you go at once to a basement or storm shelter, or to a small inside room on the lowest floor with no windows, and cover your head and neck.
explain: A hurricane in Florida brings huge winds, rain and a rise of the sea called a storm surge; you leave if told to, stay indoors away from windows, and never walk or drive through flood water, because just 15 centimetres of fast water can knock you off your feet.
explain: In an earthquake in California or New Zealand you drop to your hands and knees, take cover under a strong table and hold on until the shaking stops; if you are by the sea and the shaking was long or strong, you go to high ground at once, because a tsunami may follow.
explain: In a bushfire in Australia the safest choice is to leave early, as your family's plan says; in a Canadian blizzard you stay indoors, and if you must go out you cover your skin against frostbite.
explain: A family in Texas drove through a flooded road; the water was deeper and faster than it looked, and their car floated away until rescuers reached them.
explain: A boy in Oklahoma ignored a tornado warning and went outside to watch; flying branches and glass hurt him before he got back in.
fact: A tornado warning means a tornado has been seen or found by radar, so you take shelter at once.
fact: The safest place in a tornado is a basement or storm shelter, or an inside room on the lowest floor with no windows.
fact: A mobile home or a car is not safe in a tornado; you go to a strong building or storm shelter nearby.
fact: In a hurricane you stay indoors away from windows, or leave the area if officials tell you to.
fact: The calm eye of a hurricane is followed by strong winds again, so you stay inside.
fact: You never walk, swim or drive through flood water.
fact: In an earthquake you drop, cover and hold on; if you are outside, you first move away from walls, buildings and wires that could fall.
fact: Running outside during an earthquake is dangerous because of falling glass and bricks.
fact: Near the coast, a long or strong earthquake is a warning to go to high ground in case of a tsunami, a giant wave that an earthquake can cause.
fact: In a bushfire, leaving early is the safest choice.
fact: Bushfire smoke is harmful, so you stay indoors with windows closed or leave as your plan says.
fact: Frostbite makes skin white or greyish and numb, usually on fingers, toes, ears and nose; it is warmed gently with warm, not hot, water, never rubbed with snow or held by a fire.
fact: A family emergency kit holds water, food, a torch, batteries, a radio and first aid things; a battery radio still gives warnings when the power and phones fail.
fact: Just 6 inches of moving water can knock a person off their feet.
fact: About a foot of fast-moving water can carry away most cars.
exercise: A tornado warning sounds in Kansas. Where should a family without a basement go? | A small inside room on the lowest floor, away from windows | An upstairs bedroom with big windows, so they can see it coming | Outside, to watch where it is going | The garage, in the car
exercise: Why do you go to a room with no windows in a tornado? | Flying glass and objects can break through windows | Windows make the wind inside the room much stronger | Tornadoes are attracted to light | You need to keep the room dark
exercise: What part of the body should you protect most during a tornado? | Your head and neck | Your feet | Your hands | Your knees
exercise: What does a tornado warning mean? | A tornado has been seen or spotted on radar, so take shelter now | The weather might possibly allow a tornado to form later on, so keep watching the news | The tornado is over and it is safe | Only farmers need to take shelter
exercise: In Florida, the hurricane suddenly goes calm and the sun comes out. What should a family do? | Stay inside, because the eye passes and the winds return | Go outside to check the damage | Drive to the shops while it is calm | Open all the windows to let in fresh air now the storm has finished
exercise: Why should you never walk through flood water? | Even shallow fast water can knock you over and hide dangers | Flood water is always too cold | It ruins your shoes | Flood water is always safe to walk through as long as it is shallow
exercise: What should you do when an earthquake starts while you are in the classroom? | Drop, take cover under your desk and hold on | Run outside as fast as you can | Stand in the doorway | Stand by the window so you can see what is happening outside
exercise: Why is running outside during an earthquake dangerous? | Glass, bricks and other things can fall on you | The ground outside opens up and swallows people | Earthquakes are always stronger outside | The air outside is shaking too
exercise: A family in Wellington is at the beach when a long, strong earthquake happens. What should they do when it stops? | Go to high ground or far inland straight away | Stay to watch the waves | Wait on the beach until an official warning tells them to move | Go into the water where it is safer
exercise: What is the safest choice when a dangerous bushfire is coming towards your town in Australia? | Leave early, as the family plan says | Wait until you see flames, then decide | Go to look at the fire from a nearby hill | Hide in the garden shed until it passes
exercise: Thick bushfire smoke is covering your town. What should you do? | Stay indoors with windows and doors closed | Go for a run to get away from it | Open all the windows wide to let the smoke blow out | Breathe through your mouth outside
exercise: A girl in Manitoba must walk to a neighbour's house in a blizzard. What should she do? | Cover her skin and go with an adult | Go with bare hands so she can feel the way | Go out alone as fast as she possibly can | Wear one thin layer so she can run faster
exercise: Which are signs of frostbite? | Skin that is white or greyish and numb | Skin that is red, itchy and sweaty all over | Skin that tingles only when it is in the sun | Skin that gets a little darker after a hot bath
exercise: Which is in a good family emergency kit? | Water, food, a torch, batteries and a radio | Candles, matches and fireworks | Ice cream and fresh milk | A television and a games console
test: A boy in Oklahoma is at a friend's house in a mobile home when a tornado warning sounds. What is safest? | Go quickly to a sturdy building or storm shelter nearby | Stay in the mobile home and hide under a bed until it has passed | Hide in the car parked next to it | Go up onto the roof to escape
test: Hurricane officials tell a family in Miami to leave their home near the sea. Why should they go? | The storm surge could flood the area with seawater | Hurricanes only ever hit the houses that stand by the sea | The wind is only strong near the sea | The sea will be too rough for swimming
test: A road ahead is covered by flowing water that looks only a little deep. What should the driver do? | Turn around and find another way | Drive through quickly before it rises | Walk through it to test the depth | Drive slowly through the very middle
test: A girl in Los Angeles is in the playground when the ground starts shaking. What should she do? | Get away from walls and wires, then drop and cover her head | Run into the school building at once, where the walls will keep her safe | Climb up the climbing frame | Stand next to the school wall
test: Which is true about earthquakes? | You drop, cover and hold on until the shaking stops | Doorways are the safest place in every building, old or new | You should always run outside at once | The ground opens and swallows people
test: Why do people near the coast in New Zealand say long or strong, get gone? | A long or strong quake may cause a tsunami | The sea gets far too cold after an earthquake | Long quakes break up all the roads near the sea | Strong quakes make the tide go out for a week
test: A family in Victoria hears that the fire danger is catastrophic tomorrow. What does their bushfire plan most likely say? | Leave the area early, before a fire can start | Stay at home and keep watch for smoke from the windows | Go to the bush to see if a fire starts | Fill the bath and wait in it
test: A boy in Alberta comes in from a blizzard with numb, pale fingertips. What should happen? | Warm them gently with warm, not hot, water and tell an adult | Rub them hard with handfuls of fresh snow | Hold them right up against a hot fire | Go back out in the cold to get the feeling back
test: Why does a family keep a battery radio in their emergency kit? | Power and phones may fail, but the radio still gives warnings | A radio is a good toy for a storm | Radios send out a signal that attracts rescuers to your house | The radio charges the torch
test: tornado warning just came on!! | Shelter now, lowest floor, no windows | Go outside and watch which way it goes | Wait to see it first | Open all the windows
test: hurricane's gone calm, can we go out | No, it's the eye, the wind comes back | Yes, it's over | Yes, for a quick look round the street | Yes, if the sun is out
test: ground's shaking!! run outside? | No, drop, cover and hold on | Yes, run out fast | Stand in the window | Run down the stairs
test: road's flooded, only shallow, walk through? | No, never walk through flood water | Yes, if it's only up to your knees or lower | Yes, if you go fast | Yes, if you hold a friend
test: road's flooded but it looks shallow, walk through? | No, turn back, moving water can sweep you away | Yes, if you hold hands | Yes, if you go slowly | Yes, if you wear wellies, they keep you steady in water
test: tornado warning but i wanna see it | Go to a room inside, low down, away from windows | Go outside for a photo | Stand by a window to watch | Get in the car and chase it, you'll get an amazing video
test: Leo says floods are only dangerous if u can't swim. right? | No, fast water knocks over even strong swimmers | Yes, swimmers are always safe | Yes, floods are just big puddles that look scary | Yes, unless it is cold
test: heard a family in Kansas went outside in the eye of the hurricane, thought it was over, and the wind came back | Real lesson: after the calm eye of a hurricane the winds come back, so stay inside | Real lesson: hurricanes are over once it goes calm and the sun comes out again | It proves only Kansas families get hurricanes | It is gossip, so the calm is safe
test: my cousin says in an earthquake u should run outside as fast as u can | Wrong; drop, cover and hold on, as outside you can be hit by falling glass | He is right, outside is always safer | He is right, if you run downstairs | He is right, or stand in a doorway, as doorways never fall down in a quake
test: everyone's saying the Garcias lost everything in the flood cos they were too dumb to leave, pass it on | Do not pass it on; it is cruel, and they need kindness after a disaster | Pass it on, because it is a lesson | Pass it on, since everyone knows anyway | Pass it on, and say they were warned three times and just laughed about it
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises a tornado warning? | A tornado has been sighted or detected by radar, and shelter must be taken at once | Conditions may later favour a tornado, and no action whatsoever is required at present | A tornado has passed and it is safe to go outside | The tornado season has begun for the year
exam: Identify the safest location during a tornado. | A basement, storm shelter, or windowless interior room on the lowest floor | An upstairs room with large windows, from which the tornado may be observed | A car parked in the open | The roof of the building
exam: It being given that a strong or prolonged earthquake is felt near the coast, select the course of action consistent with established guidance. | To proceed to high ground in case of a tsunami | To go down to the beach in order to observe the sea | To remain upon the shore until the water recedes | To swim out beyond the breaking waves to safety
exam: Select the response that is consistent with established guidance concerning a bushfire. | Leaving early is the safest choice | Remaining until the flames are visible is the safest choice | Sheltering in a tree is the safest choice | Driving towards the smoke is the safest choice
